      "body": "\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nThe Step3 tool parser silently drops any parameter whose value contains `<`. This PR makes the parameter regex match up to the closing tag instead of the first `<`.\r\n\r\n## Claims\r\n\r\n- Step3 tool calls keep parameter values that contain `<`, such as `a < 5` or `<b>hi</b>`, in streaming and non-streaming mode.\r\n- Values without `<` parse exactly as before.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\n\r\nNew unit test `test_parameter_value_with_angle_bracket`, run in both streaming and non-streaming mode:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npython -m pytest -q tests/tool_parsers/test_step3_tool_parser.py\r\n```\r\n\r\n| | Result |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Before (test only) | `2 failed, 9 passed, 8 xfailed`: `assert {} == {'sql': 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE a < 5', 'html': '<b>hi</b>'}` |\r\n| After | `11 passed, 8 xfailed` |\r\n\r\nThe 8 xfails already exist on main and are unchanged.\r\n\r\nEnd-to-end check with a Step3 model (`tool_choice=\"auto\"`, a tool with a string parameter, prompt asking for a SQL query with `<`):\r\n\r\nParser check with the real Step3 tokenizer (`stepfun-ai/step3`). A tool call whose `sql` value contains `<` goes through `run_tool_extraction`, the helper the tool parser tests use, in both modes:\r\n\r\n| Mode | `main` (`arguments`) | This PR (`arguments`) |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| non-streaming | `{}` | `{\"sql\": \"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE total < 100\"}` |\r\n| streaming | `{}` | `{\"sql\": \"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE total < 100\"}` |\r\n\r\nOn `main` the call is still emitted, but its arguments come back empty, so a client runs the tool with no input.\r\n\r\nNot run end to end: Step3 has 321B parameters and I have no hardware that can serve it. The change is confined to the parameter regex, which the check above drives with the model's real tokenizer.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Reproduction script</summary>\r\n\r\n    import json\r\n    from vllm.tokenizers import get_tokenizer\r\n    from vllm.tool_parsers import ToolParserManager\r\n    from tests.tool_parsers.utils import run_tool_extraction\r\n\r\n    tok = get_tokenizer(\"stepfun-ai/step3\")\r\n    out = (\"<｜tool_calls_begin｜><｜tool_call_begin｜>function<｜tool_sep｜>\"\r\n           '<steptml:invoke name=\"run_query\">'\r\n           '<steptml:parameter name=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM orders WHERE total < 100</steptml:parameter>'\r\n           \"</steptml:invoke><｜tool_call_end｜><｜tool_calls_end｜>\")\r\n    for streaming in (False, True):\r\n        parser = ToolParserManager.get_tool_parser(\"step3\")(tok)\r\n        _, calls = run_tool_extraction(parser, out, streaming=streaming)\r\n        print(streaming, [json.loads(c.function.arguments or \"{}\") for c in calls])\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n## Details\r\n\r\nRoot cause: `_parse_steptml_invoke` matched values with `([^<]*)`, so the regex failed on any value containing `<` and the parameter was skipped without a log line. The value group is now `(.*?)` with `re.DOTALL`. It ends at the first `</steptml:parameter>`, and multi-line values still match as before.\r\n\r\nLimit: a value that contains the literal text `</steptml:parameter>` still ends early. The format has no escaping, so this cannot be fixed in the parser.\r\n\r\nNot a duplicate: I searched open and closed PRs for \"step3 tool parser\" and issues and PRs for \"steptml\". #49567 (whitespace in Step3 values) only removes `.strip()` and keeps `[^<]*`. #55115 and #57809 change streaming bookkeeping, not the regex. #55175 changes test fixtures only. No issue reports this bug.\r\n\r\nCo-author: Claude\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\r\n\r\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\r\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\r\n\r\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\r\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\r\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\r\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\r\n</details>\r\n",
      "changedFiles": 2,
